Download the Nature kinetic-profile dataset from:

https://doi.org/10.48420/16965292

Unpack the archive and place the nine `.pkl` files in this directory (`data/nature_data/`).
If the download unpacks into a nested folder, copy only the pickle files here.
Expect about 10 GB of disk space. These files are not included in the git repository.

This repository's training/eval scripts use **Python 3.10** and the packages in `Nature_Kinetic_Benchmark/requirements.txt`. The Python 3.7 / numpy 1.19 lines below are from the original Nature dataset release, not this code.

---

These files contain a training, validation and test set for the work "Organic Reaction Mechanism Classification with Machine Learning"

It contains 5,000,000 samples of kinetic profiles for reaction mechanisms transforming a substrate S into a product P, catalyzed by a catalyst, cat.
These were generated by solving the corresponding ordinary differential equations corresponding to 20 typical mechanisms.
These mechanisms belong to four distinct categories: 1) the core mechanism (M1), is the simplest Michaelis-Menten-type mechanism; 2) mechanisms with bicatalytic steps (M2-M5), involve either catalyst dimerization (M2 and M3), or a reaction between two different catalytic species (M4 and M5); 3) mechanisms with catalyst activation steps based on the core mechanism, where a precatalyst requires activation unimolecularly (M6), via substrate coordination (M7) or via ligand dissociation (M8); 4) mechanisms with a variety of catalyst deactivation steps from either catalytic intermediate of the core mechanism (M9-M20).
These kinetic samples can be used to train, validate and test a machine learning model.

### REQUIREMENTS ###
- python: 3.7.10
- numpy: 1.19.2

### INSTRUCTIONS ###
Download from https://doi.org/10.48420/16965292, unpack into this folder, and use with the scripts in this repository.

### STRUCTURE OF THE DATA ###
x1_files: numpy arrays of shape (n,4), corresponding to (number of samples, initial concentrations of catalyst).
x2_train and x2_val files: numpy arrays of shape (n, 21, 12), corresponding to (number of samples, temporal axis, [time,substrate,product] for each of four kinetic runs in each sample).
x2_test file: dictionary of dictionaries for test data with 2, 6 and 20 timepoints, and standard errors of 1, (0, 1 and 5) and 1, respectively.
			  for example x2_test_file[6][1] contains a numpy array of a test set made of 6 timepoints per kinetic trace and 1%  standard error.
y_files: the labels for each sample, as a numpy array of shape (n,1). The labels are values from 0 to 19, corresponding to mechanisms M1 to M20.
